12. What is POJO in Spring?
-->POJO = Plain Old Java Object

It is simply a normal Java class that doesn't need to extend a special framework class or implement a special framework interface.

Example: 
      public class Student {
      
          private int id;
          private String name;
      
          public Student() {
          }
      
          public Student(int id, String name) {
              this.id = id;
              this.name = name;
          }
      
          // getters and setters
      }
      
      This is a POJO.
      
      Spring can manage this POJO as a bean.

For example:  
      @Component
      public class Student {
      }
      
      Now Spring can create and manage the Student object.

Interview point:
      Spring promotes POJO-based development, which keeps business logic independent of the framework.






13. What is Dependency Management in Spring?
-->This is closely related to Dependency Injection.

Suppose:
      class Car {
          Engine engine;
      }

Here:
      Car → depends on → Engine
      
      So Engine is a dependency of Car.
      
      Instead of Car creating the Engine itself:
      
      class Car {
      
          Engine engine = new Engine();
      
      }

Spring can provide the dependency:
      
      @Component
      class Engine {
      }
      @Component
      class Car {
      
          private Engine engine;
      
          @Autowired
          public Car(Engine engine) {
              this.engine = engine;
          }
      }
      
      Spring Container creates Engine and injects it into Car.

Flow:
      Spring Container
             ↓
      Creates Engine
             ↓
      Creates Car
             ↓
      Injects Engine into Car


Interview answer:
      Dependency management in Spring means that Spring Container creates and manages objects and automatically provides the required
      dependencies to those objects through Dependency Injection.
